# The edge. Everything the internet touches arrives here first.
#
# WHY CADDY: automatic TLS, including for *.wifios.co.ke, with no certbot cron and no
# renewal that silently expires at 2am. It is one binary and one config file, which is
# the whole point — the next person to touch this should understand it in ten minutes,
# not inherit a Kubernetes cluster they did not ask for.
#
# THE ROUTING MODEL, and why it is worth understanding before changing anything:
#
#   wifios.co.ke              -> the marketing site
#   api.wifios.co.ke          -> Django. A FIXED address, because Safaricom's callbacks
#                                are registered against it and must never move.
#   admin.wifios.co.ke        -> Platform Control (Danamo's own console)
#   <slug>.wifios.co.ke       -> that ISP's console
#   <slug>.wifios.co.ke/api/* -> Django, SAME ORIGIN as the console
#   <slug>.wifios.co.ke/portal-> the captive portal that ISP's customers see
#
# The same-origin /api is the load-bearing bit. Auth is an httpOnly cookie (no browser
# storage anywhere — the hard rule of this system), and a same-origin API is what lets
# the browser attach it with no CORS, no preflight on every request, and no second
# trusted-origins list to keep in sync. Cross-origin cookies would also mean
# SameSite=None, which is a strictly worse security posture.

{
	email {$ACME_EMAIL}

	# Wildcard certificates need a DNS challenge (Let's Encrypt will not issue *.x
	# over HTTP-01). The token is scoped to DNS edits on this zone and lives in the
	# environment — never in this file, never in the repo.
	#
	# The apex and the wildcard both challenge on _acme-challenge.wifios.co.ke, so the
	# record must be fully propagated before the CA validates or it races. We check
	# against public resolvers (not the box's own) and give propagation real time.
	acme_dns cloudflare {$CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN} {
		resolvers 1.1.1.1 8.8.8.8
		propagation_delay 45s
		propagation_timeout 10m
	}

	# Staging ACME while you are still fighting DNS: Let's Encrypt's production rate
	# limits are unforgiving (5 failures/hour), and hitting them locks you out for the
	# afternoon. Uncomment, get it working, then comment out and delete the certs.
	# acme_ca https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory
}

# Headers every response gets, everywhere. Cheap, and each one closes a real class of
# attack rather than decorating a scan report.
(security_headers) {
	header {
		# We are HTTPS-only and say so for two years, including every ISP subdomain.
		# Once a browser has seen this it will not even attempt http:// — which kills
		# the "downgrade them on the coffee-shop wifi" attack outright. NB: hard to
		# undo, so it goes on LAST, once the domain is settled.
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload"

		# No framing: nobody wraps an ISP's console in an invisible iframe and farms
		# clicks off it.
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"

		# We ask for none of these EXCEPT geolocation, which the ISP console's Map genuinely
		# uses (the "you are here" dot, "use my location", the business-location pin). (self)
		# = only our own origin may request it, and the browser STILL prompts the user for
		# consent — so a compromised script can annoy, never silently locate. camera / mic /
		# payment stay fully off. One policy, set here, so every route is unambiguous.
		Permissions-Policy "camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(self), payment=()"

		# Don't advertise what we run.
		-Server
		-X-Powered-By
	}
}

# The console and portal SPAs are self-contained: their JS and CSS are bundled, and
# nothing is fetched from a CDN. So a strict CSP costs us nothing and turns "attacker
# injected a <script src=evil.com>" from a catastrophe into a blocked request.
#
# 'unsafe-inline' for styles only, because the bundlers inline critical CSS. Scripts
# get no such exemption — that is where it actually matters.
(csp_spa) {
	header Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com data:; img-src 'self' data: blob:; connect-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'"
}

# The ISP CONSOLE has the live Map (MapLibre GL). Two things the strict csp_spa blocks that
# the map genuinely needs, so this variant adds ONLY those — nothing else is loosened, and the
# money consoles (Platform Control) keep csp_spa:
#   * worker-src blob:  — MapLibre spins up its render worker from a blob: URL. Without this the
#                          map never initialises at all (not just missing tiles).
#   * the tile hosts    — the basemap is OpenStreetMap (streets) + Esri (satellite), fetched as
#                          images AND over the network, so both img-src and connect-src list them.
# (If you later self-host tiles, drop the external hosts and this collapses back to 'self'.)
(csp_console) {
	header Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; worker-src 'self' blob:; child-src 'self' blob:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com data:; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://*.tile.openstreetmap.org https://server.arcgisonline.com; connect-src 'self' https://*.tile.openstreetmap.org https://server.arcgisonline.com; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'"
}

# The Django upstream, everywhere it's proxied. Retry + active health so a rolling deploy
# (the api container restarting) never returns a hard error: Caddy HOLDS the request and
# retries for up to lb_try_duration until the api is serving again, and the health check
# keeps it from routing to an api that isn't ready yet (still migrating on boot). Combined
# with the console's own retry layer, a deploy is invisible to a logged-in ISP.
(api_backend) {
	reverse_proxy api:8000 {
		lb_try_duration 30s
		lb_try_interval 300ms
		health_uri /api/v1/health/
		health_interval 3s
		health_timeout 4s
	}
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The marketing site
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
wifios.co.ke, www.wifios.co.ke {
	import security_headers
	# The marketing site is a static Astro build. Its islands (the signup wizard) hydrate
	# via small INLINE scripts, so script-src needs 'unsafe-inline' here or the wizard
	# never boots and shows a forever-spinner. This site renders no user-controlled HTML,
	# so there is no injection point for inline script to be abused through — the risk that
	# 'unsafe-inline' normally carries doesn't exist here. The money-handling CONSOLES keep
	# the strict 'self'-only policy (their JS is all external bundles). media-src is open to
	# self for the embedded screen recordings.
	header Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com data:; img-src 'self' data:; media-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'"

	# The signup wizard talks to the API from this origin. Same-origin, so the draft
	# cookie rides along without CORS.
	handle /api/* {
		import api_backend
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy marketing:80
	}

	encode zstd gzip
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The API's own address. Safaricom's C2B validation/confirmation URLs and the STK
# callback point HERE, and they are registered with Safaricom — this hostname must not
# change without a change on their portal too.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
api.wifios.co.ke {
	import security_headers
	import api_backend
	encode zstd gzip
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Platform Control — Danamo's own console. Every ISP's money is visible from here, so
# it is the highest-value target on the platform.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
admin.wifios.co.ke {
	import security_headers
	import csp_spa

	handle /api/* {
		import api_backend
	}
	handle {
		reverse_proxy super-admin:80
	}

	encode zstd gzip
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The usage documentation — docs.wifios.co.ke. A static Astro/Starlight build with no
# API and no backend. This EXPLICIT host must sit above the *.wifios.co.ke wildcard so
# "docs" is served here, not treated as an ISP slug ("docs" is a reserved slug anyway).
# Starlight hydrates a theme toggle + search via small inline scripts, so script-src
# needs 'unsafe-inline'; the site renders no user content, so there is no injection point.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
docs.wifios.co.ke {
	import security_headers
	header Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self' data:; img-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'"
	handle {
		reverse_proxy docs:80
	}
	encode zstd gzip
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every ISP: <slug>.wifios.co.ke
#
# Django resolves the tenant from the Host header (core.tenancy._slug_from_host), so
# the subdomain IS the tenant. Reserved names (www, api, admin, portal…) are refused at
# signup — see Operator.RESERVED_SLUGS — which is what stops an ISP from registering
# the slug "api" and hijacking the callback host.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.wifios.co.ke {
	import security_headers
	import csp_console

	# Same-origin API: the cookie just works.
	handle /api/* {
		import api_backend
	}

	# Django's own surfaces (schema, admin) stay reachable per-tenant for support.
	handle /admin/* {
		import api_backend
	}
	handle /static/* {
		import api_backend
	}

	# The captive portal: what an ISP's CUSTOMERS see when they connect to the wifi.
	# The router's walled garden redirects here.
	handle /portal/* {
		uri strip_prefix /portal
		reverse_proxy portal:80
	}

	# Everything else is the ISP's console.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy admin-ui:80
	}

	encode zstd gzip
}
